"""Revised input acoustics and a careful interpretation of the existing context curves."""
import json
from html import escape as e
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];P=ROOT/'research/s1-acoustic-revision'
def load():return json.loads((P/'review.json').read_text()) if (P/'review.json').exists() else None
def render_previews(table):
 d=load()
 if not d:return ''
 body='<section id="revised-question-audio" style="scroll-margin-top:10rem"><h3>Revised question audio: room tone and microphone matching</h3><ul class="brief"><li>Continuous background replaces the old digital-silence gap.</li><li>Speech level and bounded spectral EQ follow the requester’s earlier recording in each microphone setting.</li><li>The distant/beamformed source now continues to the splice, without the old switch to a shared headset tail.</li></ul><p><strong>Input-only revisions:</strong> these are six revised realizations of the same P01 question. They have not been replayed through S1. Figures 1–4 and all saved model responses still describe the original inputs. Independent Parakeet ASR recovers the exact question in all six revisions; this checks intelligibility, not naturalness or improved answer accuracy.</p><p>Each card compares the original input at the test’s +2.92 dB question gain with two revisions. Both revisions use the requester’s recorded speech level and real, earlier annotation-gap background. The second also adds channel EQ limited to 9 dB. No new speech synthesis, voice training or artificial reverberation was used. Released AMI beamforming remains offline and may use later-recording statistics. The wording and recording cutoff stay fixed.</p><div class="array-demo-grid">'
 for name in ['headset_mix','distant_01','beamformed_mdm8']:
  tone=next(c for c in d['cases'] if c['condition']==name and c['variant']=='tone');channel=next(c for c in d['cases'] if c['condition']==name and c['variant']=='channel');body+='<article class="array-demo-card" id="revised-'+name+'"><h4>'+e(tone['label'])+'</h4>'
  for label,url,kind in [('Original input at test gain',tone['old_preview_audio'],'original'),('Revised: continuous room tone',tone['preview_audio'],'tone'),('Revised: room tone + channel EQ',channel['preview_audio'],'channel')]:body+='<p><strong>'+label+'</strong></p><audio controls preload="none" src="'+e(url)+'" aria-label="'+e(tone['label'])+' '+kind+' revised-input comparison"></audio>'
  body+='<details class="array-demo-asr"><summary>Show revised-question ASR</summary>'
  for c in [tone,channel]:body+='<p><strong>'+('Room tone' if c['variant']=='tone' else 'Room tone + EQ')+':</strong> '+e(c['input_asr']['text'])+' (external input ASR WER '+format(c['input_asr']['WER'],'.3f')+').</p>'
  body+='</details><details><summary>Compare the requester’s real recording</summary><p>Separate earlier speech, source 223.39–229.44 seconds, unused in the EQ fit.</p><audio controls preload="none" src="'+e(tone['real_reference_audio'])+'" aria-label="'+e(tone['label'])+' real requester reference"></audio></details></article>'
 body+='</div><p>The original transition contains a continuous 183.7 ms run of digital zeros. In the revisions the longest zero run in the same region is at most 0.125 ms. Real background continues through the pause and question; the pause duration itself is retained. Room tone is shuffled from earlier non-word gaps with short crossfades, rather than copied from future audio. Three background-only ASR controls produce no words.</p><p>The EQ was estimated from two earlier, paired headset/channel utterances. On a separate recorded utterance, the spectral difference falls from 3.06 to 1.66 dB for the distant microphone and from 2.73 to 2.02 dB for beamformed audio. This is a smoothed, level-normalized spectral check over 150–6,000 Hz; it does not validate synthetic-voice naturalness. A fixed room-response estimate did not generalize and was excluded. <a href="#question-transition-measurements">Acoustic measurements and controls</a> · <a href="research/s1-acoustic-revision/review.json">Audio provenance and checks</a>.</p></section>'
 body+='<section id="context-elbow-analysis" style="scroll-margin-top:10rem"><h3>Do the saved curves show an elbow?</h3><ul class="brief"><li>The headset trace shows a question-recognition change around 54–61 seconds, with later recoveries.</li><li>Distant and beamformed recognition improve again at longer contexts, so there is no common monotonic cutoff.</li><li>A response-quality elbow is not identifiable: all 33 cases with complete reference evidence miss a complete answer.</li></ul>'
 analysis=json.loads((P/'elbow-analysis.json').read_text());labels={c['condition']:c['label'] for c in d['cases']}
 body+=table('Descriptive strict question-ASR passes over scored original cases; these are recognition counts, not response-quality scores. Bins were chosen for this summary, not a fitted breakpoint.',['Recording setting','Below 60 s','60 to below 180 s','180 s and longer','Complete responses with all evidence'],[[e(labels[c['condition']])]+[str(b['strict_passes'])+'/'+str(b['WER_scored'])+(' ('+str(b['unscored'])+' unscored)' if b['unscored'] else '') for b in c['bins']]+[str(c['source_complete'])+'/'+str(c['eligible_responses'])] for c in analysis['conditions']])
 body+='<p>For headset audio, strict recognition passes 10/12 contexts below 60 seconds and 2/29 from 60 seconds onward. The first clear loss of question meaning is at 61.11 seconds, but meaning later returns at eight levels, up to 206.11 seconds. Each step also adds a different sentence group, so added content and duration change together. This is a candidate recognition change in one trace, not an established response-quality or memory limit. At that first loss the prompt uses 9,901 tokens of a 49,152-token budget, without trimming.</p><p>Only contexts of at least 198.91 seconds include every required answer fact. The shorter cases therefore cannot provide a valid before/after comparison of full answer quality. Across the 33 eligible cases, there is no high-quality response regime followed by a decline: none is complete. Question recognition is also insufficient by itself—17 eligible cases pass the strict recognition rule, yet none gives a complete grounded answer.</p><p>These tests prepend older meeting audio before a fixed question. The last reference fact still ends at source time 136.44 seconds and the synthetic-question segment starts at 309.21 seconds, leaving the same 172.77-second gap in every eligible case. The final level additionally includes 74.89 seconds of initial recording silence. This design measures sensitivity to additional earlier context; it does not trace forgetting as the delay after a fact grows.</p><p>Some apparent failures come from the strict text rule: <a href="#sweep-beamformed_mdm8-L40">beamformed level 40</a> has WER 0.0625 and preserves the request but omits “Assistant,” so it is marked a strict miss. The acoustic splice and the ten LLM/source-review disagreements are further reasons to avoid a precise elbow claim. <a href="research/s1-acoustic-revision/elbow-analysis.json">Exact descriptive analysis</a>.</p><p>A useful next test is a paired original/revised-input replay with short and long contexts while retaining all answer facts, followed by multiple questions with a controlled fact-to-question delay. The revised audio is ready for that comparison; no new S1 result is reported here.</p></section>'
 return body

def render_measurements():
 d=load()
 if not d:return ''
 a=d['accounting'];body='<details id="question-transition-measurements"><summary>Question-transition measurements, noise-only controls and resources</summary><p>Frame root-mean-square (RMS) level measures average signal amplitude in 20 ms windows. Decibels relative to full scale (dBFS) express that level relative to maximum digital amplitude; more negative values are quieter. The dotted line at zero seconds marks the end of original source audio and start of the insertion. The plot floors silence at −100 dBFS for display.</p><figure><div style="overflow-x:auto" tabindex="0"><img src="research/s1-acoustic-revision/transition.svg" style="width:100%;min-width:850px;background:white" alt="Original and revised input RMS around the source-to-question splice for headset, distant and beamformed audio"></div><figcaption>Figure 5. Original and revised input amplitude around the splice. The revision combines channel-continuous source audio, recorded room tone, measured speech level and bounded EQ; it is not a single-factor accuracy experiment. <a href="research/s1-acoustic-revision/transition.svg">SVG</a> · <a href="research/s1-acoustic-revision/transition.png">PNG</a>.</figcaption></figure>'
 for c in [c for c in d['cases'] if c['variant']=='channel']:body+='<p><strong>'+e(c['label'])+' background-only control:</strong> external ASR returns no words.</p><audio controls preload="none" src="'+e(c['background_control_audio'])+'" aria-label="'+e(c['label'])+' revised background-only control"></audio>'
 body+='<p>All original source-prefix samples are preserved after the existing fixed per-channel gain. Only past source audio and the existing synthetic question are used; reference-answer text is not part of the revised input. '+e('ASR job '+a['job']+' used one L40S 48 GB: Parakeet on GPU 0, reused sequentially with no S1/TTS/S2/judge sharing; CPU preparation and source checks came first. Maximum one GPU active, currently zero. Allocation '+str(a['elapsed_seconds'])+' seconds / '+format(a['gpu_hours'],'.6f')+' GPU hours; offline throughput '+format(a['audio_seconds_per_allocation_second'],'.3f')+' seconds of audio per allocation-second, not response latency.')+'</p><p><strong>Scope:</strong> one speaker, one question and one meeting; the spectral comparison was a development check, and no independent human listening rating was collected. <a href="research/s1-acoustic-revision/manifest.json">Checksums</a>.</p></details>'
 return body
